The problem asks us to count the number of distinct pairs of mochi $(A_i, A_j)$ such that $A_i$ can be placed on top of $A_j$.
The condition for placing mochi $A$ (size $a$) on top of mochi $B$ (size $b$) is $a \le \frac{b}{2}$.
We are given $N$ mochi with sizes $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_N$, sorted in ascending order ($A_i \le A_{i+1}$).
We need to find the number of pairs $(i, j)$ with $i \neq j$ such that $A_i$ can be placed on top of $A_j$.
Wait, the problem says "Two kagamimochi are distinguished if at least one of the mochi is different, even if the sizes of the mochi are the same."
This implies that we are counting pairs of indices $(i, j)$ such that $A_i$ is placed on $A_j$.
However, usually "different kinds" in competitive programming problems involving arrays with duplicates might imply distinct values or distinct indices.
